re=['The new unprofiled figures give a caller requirement of **'+F(calc[patterns[0]]['none_serial_budget_us'])+' µs/request** and **'+F(calc[patterns[1]]['none_serial_budget_us'])+' µs/send** at 0.6. The caller currently uses **'+F(calc[patterns[0]]['none_caller_us'])+' / '+F(calc[patterns[1]]['none_caller_us'])+' µs**. This is approximately **'+f"{100*(1-calc[patterns[0]]['none_serial_budget_us']/calc[patterns[0]]['none_caller_us']):.1f}"+'% / '+f"{100*(1-calc[patterns[1]]['none_serial_budget_us']/calc[patterns[1]]['none_caller_us']):.1f}"+'%** caller reduction, before giving other serial resources any headroom. The earlier three-run reference requires 6.752 / 2.701 µs; the conclusion is not dependent on choosing the easier of the two cohorts.', '', 'The new unprofiled target application drain consumes **18.663 µs/request / 5.805 µs/send**, or about **0.935 / 0.966 cores** in its captured windows. The source caller uses about **0.935 / 0.806 cores**. Thus request is constrained at both ends; in the send capture the owner-wide target drain is nearer saturation than the caller. The final profiles also show source receive 14.823 µs/request, target receive 14.586 / 4.320 µs, and Core I/O demands above the same serial budget. **A source-only thin API cannot establish 0.6 for either end-to-end cell.**', '', 'With the current measured native/codec work frozen, the answer is **no**: even the intentionally excessive deletion model in §3 does not give 0.6. With cheaper representations, cheaper required actions and changed *natural* batching, current contracts leave the answer **open**. Reaching 0.6 would require those effects to be measured, not assumed. No clause specifies that each native API call must consume today’s CPU, and no current data establishes the best achievable version of those calls.']
